WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:06.680
I was 15 feet from this guy.
But I was safely in in a Land Rover.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:10.900
If I had not been I would have been a little stressed out.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:17.020
My adrenal cortex would have been squirting out cortisol the stress hormone

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:21.080
as I saw this guy. This is an adaptive response.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:25.280
We undergo some sort of acute stressor.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:30.380
Our adrenal glands go into action: they squirt cortisol.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:35.360
We get in a physiological state that prepares us for the sorts of things

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.900
that we're likely to have to do to adapt to the stressor.

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:44.780
One of the truly remarkable things about reading a story

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:48.900
(when you're engaged with a story)

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:58.680
you get a major activation of your HPA access,
your Hypothalamus Pituitary Adrenal axis.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.080
This is the classic stress response.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:06.520
It's the system that regulates stress, but

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:12.920
it's also essential in the control of
the immune system, mood, sexuality and energy.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:15.160
And I think that this is the foundation

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:17.380
the physiological foundation of that feeling

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:20.200
when you're engaged in a story
and you're really nervous.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:25.500
I mean reading "Flight Behavior," I'm just so anxious about what's gonna happen to Dellarobia.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:32.000
What new humiliation of being poor in America
is she gonna have to endure?

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:35.220
And it's that feeling of needing to turn the page.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:38.540
This is this activation of the HPA axis.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:43.120
It's cool. Is it useful instrumentally?

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:47.440
And I have to give props to my
Stanford colleague, Robert Sapolsky.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:52.600
Many of you are probably familiar.
He's an incredible public speaker.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:56.620
And just a huge intellect.

00:01:56.620 --> 00:02:00.540
He has been a good mentor to me.

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:04.300
Why do we care about cortisol,
other than it's kind of cool

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:06.980
that you get this physiological response
to reading a story?

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:11.200
It turns out that stress opens people's minds
to behavior change.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.520
The right kind of stress opens people's minds
to behavior change.

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:22.000
University College London psychologist / neuroscientist Tali Sharot

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:28.420
has a fairly recent book on changing people's minds ("The Influential Mind").

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:34.640
And she's quite famous for her technical work
on what's called a positivity bias.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:40.500
You're faced with information
that comes at you from the world,

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:44.360
you have priors, prior beliefs,
about what the world is like

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:49.760
And when you get information that's good news,
relative to your desires,

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:53.840
you take that and you say
'Good, my hypothesis is supported!'

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:59.720
And when you get bad news, you kind of
ignore it. You don't pay as much attention to it.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:01.920
Which seems a little paradoxical

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:06.580
It seems like for a survival algorithm
you'd want to learn more from bad news.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:08.960
That's the way I think about these things.

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:14.640
But it turns out that isn't the way
a lot of people go about doing it.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:18.880
Until you put them under stress.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:23.380
When you put people under stress when you give them...

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:26.300
one of these psychologists' funny things that you do:

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:30.540
you tell them they're going to have to give
a public lecture after they do your test

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:31.800
or something like that.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:36.080
It turns out you open people's minds
to both good news and bad news.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:42.380
And they behave like proper Bayesians.
They update their priors in a rational kind of way.

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:49.660
Isn't that cool? And that means
they've had their HPA axis activated.

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:52.820
It means they're got that cortisol response.

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:57.080
And that's exactly what good fiction does to you.

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:07.200
Is it possible that there's a physiological foundation for good fiction changing people's valence bias?

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:12.400
Their willingness to accept good and bad news?
It's a hypothesis.

